"Machine Learning (https://arxiv.org/list/cs.LG/new)\nciteturn41283view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/list/cs.LG/new\",\"pattern\":\"2610.23048\"}); Total lines: 5005\nNo matching text found for \"2610.23048\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMachine Learning (https://arxiv.org/list/cs.LG/new)\nciteturn41283view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/list/cs.LG/new\",\"pattern\":\"2610.23051\"}); Total lines: 5005\nNo matching text found for \"2610.23051\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMachine Learning (https://arxiv.org/list/cs.LG/new)\nciteturn41283view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/list/cs.LG/new\",\"pattern\":\"2610.23213\"}); Total lines: 5005\nNo matching text found for \"2610.23213\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nArtificial Intelligence (https://arxiv.org/list/cs.AI/new)\nciteturn41283view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/list/cs.AI/new\",\"pattern\":\"2610.23048\"}); Total lines: 4424\nL647: Rust is increasingly adopted as a memory-safe successor to C, and transpilers such as \\crust{} can migrate entire C projects while preserving their behavior. Preserving behavior, however, does not guarantee preserving performance. We study this gap on 20 real-world C projects and their \\crust{} translations. On the eight projects used for analysis, the translated Rust is more than 3\\% slower than the original C on 12 of 19 operations, by up to 62.2\\%.\nL648: By comparing execution-cost profiles, optimized LLVM IR, and compiler optimization remarks across each C--Rust pair, we trace these slowdowns to nine recurring performance issues, spanning residual runtime work, missed compiler optimizations, and C-style source patterns, and derive an optimization direction for each. We then present \\ourtool{}, an LLM-based approach that encodes these issues as reusable optimization rules to recover the performance of translated Rust.\nL649: \\ourtool{} profiles each project to locate hot functions and matches them against rule-specific patterns. The LLM then checks each rule's rewrite preconditions against the project context before rewriting, and a rewrite is kept only if it passes both functional and performance validation. On 41 operations from twelve held-out projects, \\ourtool{} preserves C outputs on all tested workloads and speeds up 40 operations over the \\crust{} translations, 18 of which end up faster than the original C.\nL650: It outperforms the general-purpose coding agent \\codex{} on 31 of the 37 operations where both preserve C outputs, and outperforms its own variant without rule guidance on 34 of the 41 operations.\nL651: [50] cite455†arXiv:2610.23048 [cite456†pdf , cite457†html , cite458†other ]\nL652: \nL653: \nL654: Title: Distill Until Divergence: Error-Truncated Hindsight Distillation for Agentic RL\nL655: \nL656: cite219†Yibin Huang , cite459†Conghui Zhu L657: \nL658: Subjects: Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL659: Long-horizon interaction requires agents to make multiple decisions in a changing environment. Outcome-based GRPO lacks fine-grained guidance for intermediate decisions. Recent methods distill completed trajectories into reusable skills and use hindsight-conditioned OPD to provide dense token-level credit.\nL660: However, skill-induced probability shifts do not necessarily represent reward-aligned credit; especially after an erroneous action changes subsequent interaction states, skill-conditioned supervision may become mismatched with the current state.\nL661: To address this mismatch, we propose \\methodname{}, which routes two complementary hindsight skills according to trajectory outcomes and error boundaries: Positive skills capture successful workflows to reinforce successful trajectories and pre-error decisions, while Negative skills capture failure-specific corrections to target decisive errors. The post-error suffix is optimized exclusively by GRPO, retaining outcome-based learning without extending potentially mismatched hindsight supervision.\nL662: Extensive experiments demonstrate competitive task performance and support effective fine-grained credit allocation.\nL663: [51] cite460†arXiv:2610.23051 [cite461†pdf , cite462†html , cite463†other ]\nL664: \nL665: \nL666: Title: Agentic In-Context Learning: What Algorithms Can Transformers Learn in Closed-Loop Interaction?\nL667: \nL668: cite464†Shiguang Wu , cite46†Yaqing Wang , cite465†Quanming Yao L669: \nL670: Subjects: Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL671: The learning algorithm view of in-context learning (ICL) studies how fixed-parameter Transformers learn from supplied examples. Language model agents extend this setting through interaction: their actions determine what evidence enters context next. We call this regime Agentic In-Context Learning (Agentic ICL). Existing studies largely depend on externally supplied contexts or reward-bearing interaction histories, while work on general tool-using agents lacks a learning algorithm view.\nL672: We represent an interactive learner by its state, update, and decision rule, covering active learning, bandits, and online reinforcement learning (RL). For expressivity, we construct causal Transformers that approximate these algorithms over a finite horizon, and bound the resulting loss in return. For training, we characterize when optimizing process rewards preserves behavior that maximizes the final outcome.\nL673: Distillation experiments show that Transformers can learn to execute three representative recursions. We then train models from outcome or process rewards without oracle action or state targets, as in RL post-training. Different rewards yield distinct decision policies. Improving the alignment of process supervision with outcome-optimal action preferences shifts learned decisions accordingly. Such behavioral differences persist in a pretrained language model across textual paraphrases.\nL674: [52] cite466†arXiv:2610.23085 [cite467†pdf , cite468†html , cite469†other ]\nL675: \nL676: \nL677: Title: Rethinking GRPO for Large Reasoning Model Safety Alignment\nL678: \nL679: cite307†Jiawei Chen , cite15†Yi Zhang , cite470†Jiawei Du , cite471†Zhonghou Lv , cite472†Tairan Chen , cite473†Junyuan Shang , cite474†Jinchang Hou , cite392†Xiao Yang , cite475†Yu Tian , cite476†Chenfu Bao , cite121†Chao Yu , cite477†Zhaoxia Yin L680: \nL681: Subjects: Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nArtificial Intelligence (https://arxiv.org/list/cs.AI/new)\nciteturn41283view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/list/cs.AI/new\",\"pattern\":\"2610.23051\"}); Total lines: 4424\nL650: It outperforms the general-purpose coding agent \\codex{} on 31 of the 37 operations where both preserve C outputs, and outperforms its own variant without rule guidance on 34 of the 41 operations.\nL651: [50] cite455†arXiv:2610.23048 [cite456†pdf , cite457†html , cite458†other ]\nL652: \nL653: \nL654: Title: Distill Until Divergence: Error-Truncated Hindsight Distillation for Agentic RL\nL655: \nL656: cite219†Yibin Huang , cite459†Conghui Zhu L657: \nL658: Subjects: Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL659: Long-horizon interaction requires agents to make multiple decisions in a changing environment. Outcome-based GRPO lacks fine-grained guidance for intermediate decisions. Recent methods distill completed trajectories into reusable skills and use hindsight-conditioned OPD to provide dense token-level credit.\nL660: However, skill-induced probability shifts do not necessarily represent reward-aligned credit; especially after an erroneous action changes subsequent interaction states, skill-conditioned supervision may become mismatched with the current state.\nL661: To address this mismatch, we propose \\methodname{}, which routes two complementary hindsight skills according to trajectory outcomes and error boundaries: Positive skills capture successful workflows to reinforce successful trajectories and pre-error decisions, while Negative skills capture failure-specific corrections to target decisive errors. The post-error suffix is optimized exclusively by GRPO, retaining outcome-based learning without extending potentially mismatched hindsight supervision.\nL662: Extensive experiments demonstrate competitive task performance and support effective fine-grained credit allocation.\nL663: [51] cite460†arXiv:2610.23051 [cite461†pdf , cite462†html , cite463†other ]\nL664: \nL665: \nL666: Title: Agentic In-Context Learning: What Algorithms Can Transformers Learn in Closed-Loop Interaction?\nL667: \nL668: cite464†Shiguang Wu , cite46†Yaqing Wang , cite465†Quanming Yao L669: \nL670: Subjects: Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL671: The learning algorithm view of in-context learning (ICL) studies how fixed-parameter Transformers learn from supplied examples. Language model agents extend this setting through interaction: their actions determine what evidence enters context next. We call this regime Agentic In-Context Learning (Agentic ICL). Existing studies largely depend on externally supplied contexts or reward-bearing interaction histories, while work on general tool-using agents lacks a learning algorithm view.\nL672: We represent an interactive learner by its state, update, and decision rule, covering active learning, bandits, and online reinforcement learning (RL). For expressivity, we construct causal Transformers that approximate these algorithms over a finite horizon, and bound the resulting loss in return. For training, we characterize when optimizing process rewards preserves behavior that maximizes the final outcome.\nL673: Distillation experiments show that Transformers can learn to execute three representative recursions. We then train models from outcome or process rewards without oracle action or state targets, as in RL post-training. Different rewards yield distinct decision policies. Improving the alignment of process supervision with outcome-optimal action preferences shifts learned decisions accordingly. Such behavioral differences persist in a pretrained language model across textual paraphrases.\nL674: [52] cite466†arXiv:2610.23085 [cite467†pdf , cite468†html , cite469†other ]\nL675: \nL676: \nL677: Title: Rethinking GRPO for Large Reasoning Model Safety Alignment\nL678: \nL679: cite307†Jiawei Chen , cite15†Yi Zhang , cite470†Jiawei Du , cite471†Zhonghou Lv , cite472†Tairan Chen , cite473†Junyuan Shang , cite474†Jinchang Hou , cite392†Xiao Yang , cite475†Yu Tian , cite476†Chenfu Bao , cite121†Chao Yu , cite477†Zhaoxia Yin L680: \nL681: Subjects: Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL682: Large reasoning models (LRMs) exhibit strong reasoning capabilities but remain vulnerable to unsafe outputs. While Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) is widely used for safety alignment, its group-relative optimization may provide insufficient safety-directed learning signals. In this paper, we identify two failure modes: advantage collapse, where homogeneous rollout groups yield vanishing advantages, and unsafe diversification, where unsafe behaviors persist and become increasingly diverse.\nL683: Our theoretical analysis reveals that behavioral consistency weakens within-group safety contrasts, while entropy regularization can diversify unsafe responses when relative signals vanish. To address these challenges, we propose Beacon, comprising Safety Signal Recovery (SSR) and Safety-Utility Decoupled Alignment (SUDA).\nL684: SSR combines advantage-guided trajectory filtering and safe reference supervision to recover effective safety learning signals, while SUDA employs category-aware rewards and benign-only KL anchoring to mitigate over-refusal without compromising safety.\nL685: Experiments across four Qwen3 and DeepSeek-R1-Distill variants and eight benchmarks show that our framework achieves the best average safety and reasoning performance, reducing average safety error by up to 32.83 percentage points while maintaining low over-refusal.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nArtificial Intelligence (https://arxiv.org/list/cs.AI/new)\nciteturn41283view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/list/cs.AI/new\",\"pattern\":\"2610.23213\"}); Total lines: 4424\nL786: Collecting and labeling diverse test datasets for evaluating Deep Neural Networks (DNNs) is costly and time-consuming. This has motivated coverage-guided fuzzing techniques that generate defect-revealing corner cases from limited test data. However, their effectiveness depends heavily on configuration choices, particularly coverage criteria and seed-selection strategies.\nL787: We present a configuration-oriented analysis and decision guidelines for scalable DNN fuzzing targeting two bottlenecks: inefficient seed utilization and the cost of coverage guidance. Our analysis shows that existing seed-selection strategies repeatedly prioritize a small subset of inputs, leaving many collected seeds unexplored.\nL788: To address this issue, we propose Equalized Sampling, a constraint-based strategy that increases mean seed queue utilization to 77%, improving on prior approaches by 13 percentage points.\nL789: We further evaluate 84 fuzzing configurations on four benchmark datasets: MNIST, SVHN, GTSRB, and CIFAR-100. Results show that the non-structural criteria LSCD, LSC, and MDSC achieve coverage outcomes comparable to structural and alternative non-structural criteria while requiring substantially less computation. These criteria also promote broad behavioral exploration of the DNN under test, reducing the need to monitor multiple coverage metrics.\nL790: Among them, LSCD provides a modest but consistent runtime advantage.\nL791: Finally, we show that retraining with generated corner cases improves generalization robustness by up to 77 percentage points on average without degrading performance on the original test distribution. Together, these findings provide a practical blueprint for selecting fuzzing configurations that balance efficiency, behavioral exploration, and model hardening.\nL792: [61] cite549†arXiv:2610.23213 [cite550†pdf , cite551†html , cite552†other ]\nL793: \nL794: \nL795: Title: Not All Test-Time Compute Is Evidence: Model-Specific Bias in LLM Reasoning\nL796: \nL797: cite553†Ming Shen , cite554†Tianjun Yao L798: \nL799: Subjects: Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL800: Test-time scaling methods implicitly assume that additional computation from the same model provides independent evidence. We show that this assumption fails within a single reasoning model: errors are correlated across repeated samples, across reasoning depth, and in the model's own self-evaluation, so more compute from the same model is not more evidence.\nL801: We introduce DIA (Disagreement-triggered Independent Arbitration), which treats disagreement between an intermediate-stage and the final-stage prediction as a per-question signal of reasoning instability and resolves it by asking a small panel of heterogeneous models to solve the question independently, without seeing either candidate, and aggregating their answers directly.\nL802: Across seven benchmarks and two base models, DIA outperforms full chain-of-thought in 12 of 14 settings, including a 7.00% gain on MuSiQue, while activating on only $\\sim 17\\%$ of examples and using up to 47.2% fewer tokens than a multi-agent baseline. Ablations show that both independent solving and solver heterogeneity are necessary for these gains.\nL803: [62] cite555†arXiv:2610.23226 [cite556†pdf , cite557†other ]\nL804: \nL805: \nL806: Title: Characterising and Analysing Retrieval-Augmented Generation Techniques\nL807: \nL808: cite558†Emmanuel Igbekele , cite559†Olawande Daramola L809: \nL810: Subjects: Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL811: The advent of Large Language Models (LLMs) across many application domains has increased the need for Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) to reduce hallucinations and improve LLM performance. Thus far, several RAG techniques with impressive features and capabilities have been proposed in the literature, making it a herculean task to select the most appropriate RAG techniques for specific problem scenarios.\nL812: Thus, we propose a conceptual framework comprising main dimensions and sub-dimensions for characterising (defining \"what it is\") and analysing (defining \"how well it does\") existing RAG techniques. The conceptual framework was developed through a qualitative analysis of the attributes of RAG techniques reported in the literature.\nL813: It can serve as a pre-empirical filtering mechanism for researchers and practitioners in selecting the highest-fit RAG candidates that meet specific user and task requirements before empirical benchmarking of RAG techniques, which can be computationally and financially expensive.\nL814: [63] cite560†arXiv:2610.23256 [cite561†pdf , cite562†html , cite563†other ]\nL815: \nL816: \nL817: Title: Beyond Reward Scoring: Online Multi-Agent Preference Learning for Multimodal Response Generation\nL818: \nL819: cite564†Ziyun Zou , cite565†Jiwoo Hong , cite566†Yifan Lu , cite567†Shao Tang , cite568†Weiyi Wu , cite569†Aman Gupta , cite570†Jin Tian , cite571†Imran Razzak L820: \nL821: Comments: 32 pages, 4 figures\nL822: \nL823: Subjects: Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL824: Multimodal preference alignment often follows a score-based RLHF pipeline: a reward model is first trained to learn human preferences, then used to score on-policy responses and optimize the response model's generation. This turns preference supervision into a scalar signal for generation: a model may learn to produce answers with higher preference scores, but is rarely trained explicitly to learn the reasons underlying human preferences.\n"
